A=Author('23');add=A.add
add('5.2.3-01','Resistance measures opposition to charge flow and is quantified as potential difference divided by current.','Its unit is the ohm, Ω; an ohmmeter can measure it in an appropriate isolated unpowered component.','A larger resistance gives less current for the same applied potential difference. Do not connect an ordinary resistance-meter mode to a powered circuit. A resistor transfers electrical energy; it does not consume charge. The relation R = V/I can describe an operating point even when the component is not ohmic.','Define electrical resistance in terms of voltage and current.','Resistance is potential difference divided by current.')
add('5.2.3-02','Wire resistance depends on material, length, cross-sectional area and temperature.','For the same material and temperature, R is proportional to length and inversely proportional to cross-sectional area.','Compare like materials and avoid changing temperature while testing geometry. A longer wire gives a longer interaction path, while a thicker wire provides more conducting paths. Typical metal resistance rises with temperature. Semiconductor components can behave differently, so the temperature statement here is conditioned on metal wire.','Explain how making a wire twice as long and twice the cross-sectional area affects resistance at fixed material and temperature.','Link length to a doubling of resistance and area to a halving.~The two factors cancel, leaving the same resistance in the model.')
add('5.2.3-02-M01','For a uniform wire at fixed material, area and temperature, resistance is proportional to length.','Doubling the length doubles resistance under those conditions.','A fair experiment varies the distance between contacts on the same uniform wire. Short readings and small currents reduce warming. Measure the conducting length between contacts rather than the whole spool. Contact resistance can create an offset, especially for short wire lengths.','Explain why a longer uniform wire has greater resistance at unchanged area and temperature.','Charge carriers encounter a longer path through the material.~The resistance increases in proportion to length in this model.')
add('5.2.3-02-M02','For fixed material, length and temperature, wire resistance is inversely proportional to cross-sectional area.','Doubling diameter quadruples circular area and reduces resistance to one quarter in this model.','The diameter-area distinction matters because A = πd²/4. A twofold diameter change is not merely twofold area. A thicker conductor provides more parallel conducting paths through the same length, explaining the lower resistance qualitatively.','Explain why increasing a wire’s cross-sectional area reduces resistance when other conditions stay fixed.','The greater area offers more parallel paths for charge flow.~A larger total current can flow at the same voltage, giving lower R = V/I.')
add('5.2.3-02-M03','A typical metal wire’s resistance increases as its temperature rises.','Greater lattice vibration increases electron scattering in the simple account.','A filament lamp warms substantially as current increases, so its resistance is not constant across its full operating range. This does not mean every semiconductor has the same temperature trend. Temperature control is required when testing a fixed metal’s simple proportional current-voltage relation.','Explain why warming a metal wire commonly raises its resistance.','The lattice vibrates more strongly.~Increased scattering of mobile electrons opposes their drift more strongly.')
add('5.2.4-01','An ohmic conductor at fixed temperature has current proportional to voltage.','Its current-voltage graph is a straight line through the origin.','Use voltage on the horizontal axis and current on the vertical axis. Points (0 V, 0 A), (1 V, 0.1 A), (2 V, 0.2 A) and (3 V, 0.3 A) describe a 10 Ω resistor. On these axes the gradient is 1/R, not R. If voltage is plotted vertically against current, gradient instead gives R.','Draw current vertically against voltage for a 10 Ω ohmic resistor at fixed temperature over 0–3 V.','Plot a straight line through (0,0), (1 V,0.1 A), (2 V,0.2 A) and (3 V,0.3 A).~Label the axes and note that its gradient is 0.10 A/V, reciprocal resistance.')
A.plot('5.2.4-01',[0,1,2,3],{'10 Ω resistor':[0,.1,.2,.3]},'Voltage / V','Current / A','Ohmic resistor at fixed temperature')
add('5.2.4-02','Ohm’s law states that current is proportional to potential difference for an ohmic conductor when temperature and other physical conditions remain constant.','A temperature change can alter the resistance and invalidate one constant proportionality across the readings.','A metal wire can show a straight low-current graph while remaining cool, then depart from it when heating matters. State the fixed conditions alongside the proportionality. The equation R = V/I defines an operating-point resistance more widely; using it alone does not prove the component obeys Ohm’s law.','Explain why temperature must be controlled in an Ohm’s-law test.','Ohm’s law requires fixed temperature and other physical conditions.~Heating can change resistance, so current no longer remains proportional to voltage with one constant ratio.')
add('5.2.4-03','Resistance is potential difference across a component divided by current through it.','One ohm equals one volt per ampere.','The voltage and current must refer to the same component and operating point. For a lamp, the ratio can change as it heats. A graph gradient equals resistance only with the appropriate axes and a suitable linear relation; it is not a substitute for checking what is plotted.','Define resistance using potential difference and current.','R = V/I: potential difference across the component divided by current through it.')
add('5.2.4-04','An operating-point resistance is R = V/I.','Use volts and amperes for ohms.','A 2.4 V drop with a 0.20 A current gives 12 Ω. Convert milliamperes first: 200 mA is 0.20 A. Rearranging gives V = IR and I = V/R when using the appropriate resistance at that condition.','Calculate resistance for 3.0 V across a component carrying 0.25 A.','R = V/I = 3.0/0.25.~The resistance is 12 Ω.')
add('5.2.4-05','A voltmeter across a conductor and ammeter in series provide the V and I needed for R = V/I.','Low currents and short measurement intervals help keep metal temperature approximately fixed.','Use a current-limited low-voltage source, switch and variable resistor. Set a modest current, record the two readings, then vary the setting and repeat. Open the switch between readings if heating is a concern. Plot the selected characteristic with labelled axes and use its slope consistently. The instruments ideally disturb the circuit little, but real meter effects contribute uncertainty.','Describe an experiment to measure metal-conductor resistance with an ammeter and voltmeter.','Connect the ammeter in series and voltmeter across the conductor, take paired low-current readings at near-fixed temperature and calculate V/I.')
add('5.2.4-06','Ohmic and non-ohmic components have different current-voltage characteristics.','A fixed-temperature ohmic resistor gives a straight origin line, while lamp heating and diode directionality produce different curves.','Keep the axes consistent when comparing. A lamp current-versus-voltage curve becomes shallower as its hot resistance increases. A diode has little reverse current in the intended safe range and a strongly increasing forward current. This course uses observed semiconductor behaviour without junction theory.','Explain using current-voltage graphs how an ohmic resistor differs from a filament lamp and a diode.','Link the resistor to a straight origin line at fixed temperature.~The lamp curve changes slope with heating, while the diode characteristic is strongly directional.')
add('5.2.4-06-M01','An ohmic current-voltage characteristic is linear through the origin at fixed temperature.','A non-ohmic characteristic does not maintain one constant current-to-voltage ratio over the stated range.','A larger current-versus-voltage slope indicates lower resistance for two straight ohmic lines on identical axes. The opposite comparison applies if voltage is vertical. Specify axes before interpreting steepness.','Explain using a current-voltage graph how an ohmic characteristic differs from a non-ohmic one.','The ohmic graph is a straight origin line under fixed conditions.~A non-ohmic component does not keep that same constant ratio across the range.')
add('5.2.4-06-M02','A filament lamp heats as its operating current increases.','Its increasing resistance makes the current-versus-voltage curve flatten compared with a fixed-resistance line.','For positive voltage, the slope becomes smaller at larger voltage in the illustrative characteristic. A similar symmetric behaviour occurs for reversed polarity in the simple filament model. Unlike an ideal fixed-temperature resistor, the lamp’s physical temperature is changing during the sweep.','Explain using a current-voltage graph why a filament lamp differs from a fixed-temperature ohmic resistor.','The lamp curve becomes shallower as voltage increases, whereas the ohmic graph stays straight.~Heating raises the filament resistance and changes the ratio.')
add('5.2.4-06-M03','A diode’s observed characteristic favours current in one direction.','A resistor can conduct in either direction without the same strong rectifying asymmetry.','Use a safe limited-current model and keep reverse voltage within the component’s intended range. The forward curve rises steeply after its transition region, while reverse current is small in the depicted range. A zero reverse current is an ideal approximation rather than a universal exact claim. No junction or doping theory is required here.','Explain using a current-voltage graph how a diode differs from a resistor.','The diode passes appreciable current mainly in its forward direction.~A simple resistor instead conducts in both directions with the relevant characteristic, so the diode can rectify.')
for key in ['5.2.4-06','5.2.4-06-M01','5.2.4-06-M02','5.2.4-06-M03']:
 A.plot(key,[-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3],{'Ohmic fixed T':[-.3,-.2,-.1,0,.1,.2,.3],'Lamp illustrative':[-.18,-.15,-.10,0,.10,.15,.18],'Diode illustrative':[-.001,-.001,-.001,0,.08,.22,.40]},'Voltage / V','Current / A','Illustrative characteristics; values are invented')
add('5.3-01','Circuit diagrams use standard symbols joined by lines representing conducting connections.','A diagram shows electrical topology rather than the physical shape of wires.','A cell, switch and lamp can form a series loop, with an ammeter inserted in that loop and a voltmeter connected across the lamp. Crossing wires need a clear connection convention; show a dot when a junction is intended. The symbol constructions below cover the listed components. Actual mains wiring is not a classroom construction exercise.','Explain using a circuit diagram why an ammeter is in series and a voltmeter in parallel with a resistor load.','Link series placement to the same branch current passing through the ammeter.~The voltmeter connects to the lamp’s two endpoints to compare their potentials.')
# Original symbol geometry from named coordinates; no copied artwork.
def g(key,segments,circles=None,labels=None,title='Circuit symbol'):
 A.geometry(key,[dict(points=p,color='black') if isinstance(p,list) else p for p in segments],circles,labels,title)
add('5.3-01-M01','A cell symbol has one long and one short parallel line.','A battery symbol repeats paired lines; the long line marks the positive terminal.','Connecting leads extend away from the two plates in the symbol. Draw the plates with unequal lengths so polarity is clear. A rectangle is a resistor symbol, not a substitute for a cell. For series-aiding batteries, connect the negative end of one cell to the positive end of the next.','Draw a cell symbol and a battery of two cells, indicating positive terminals.','Use unequal parallel long and short lines for each cell.~Repeat the pair for a battery and label each long-line side positive.')
g('5.3-01-M01',[[[-3,0],[-2.5,0]],[[-2.5,-.6],[-2.5,.6]],[[-2.2,-.3],[-2.2,.3]],[[-2.2,0],[-1.6,0]],[[0,0],[.5,0]],[[.5,-.6],[.5,.6]],[[.8,-.3],[.8,.3]],[[.8,0],[1.3,0]],[[1.3,-.6],[1.3,.6]],[[1.6,-.3],[1.6,.3]],[[1.6,0],[2.2,0]]],labels=[[-2.9,.8,'+  Cell'],[.3,.8,'+  Battery']],title='Cell and two-cell battery')
add('5.3-01-M02','An AC source can be shown by a circle containing a wave mark.','An open switch has a gap; a closed switch joins its contacts.','Draw a switch as two contacts and a movable arm. Its open position breaks the path, whereas the closed arm completes it. The AC symbol indicates alternating output rather than an ordinary positive-negative cell pair. Use labelled circuit models rather than connecting a classroom drawing to a mains supply.','Draw symbols for an AC source, an open switch and a closed switch.','Show a circle with a wave mark for AC.~Show separated contacts with a raised arm for open, and a connected arm for closed.')
g('5.3-01-M02',[[[-4,0],[-3.5,0]],[[-2.5,0],[-2,0]],[[-.8,0],[0,0]],[[0,0],[.8,.5]],[[1,0],[1.5,0]],[[2.3,0],[3,0]],[[3,0],[4,0]],[[4,0],[4.5,0]]],circles=[dict(centre=[-3,0],radius=.5)],labels=[[-3.2,-.1,'~'],[-.2,-.7,'Open'],[2.8,-.7,'Closed']],title='AC source and switching')
add('5.3-01-M03','A fixed resistor can be drawn as a rectangle with a lead at each end.','A diagonal arrow across the resistor indicates a variable resistance.','The arrow is a control indicator, not a current arrow through a wire. In rheostat use, a selected portion of a resistance track is included in the circuit. Symbols must show where wires connect so the variable control is not mistaken for a disconnected annotation.','Draw fixed and variable resistor symbols.','Draw a rectangle with end leads for the fixed resistor.~Add a diagonal adjusting arrow across a second resistor for the variable type.')
g('5.3-01-M03',[[[-3,0],[-2.5,0]],[[-2.5,-.3],[-1.5,-.3],[-1.5,.3],[-2.5,.3],[-2.5,-.3]],[[-1.5,0],[-1,0]],[[0,0],[.5,0]],[[.5,-.3],[1.5,-.3],[1.5,.3],[.5,.3],[.5,-.3]],[[1.5,0],[2,0]],dict(points=[[.3,-.6],[1.7,.6]],arrow=True)],labels=[[-2.5,-.8,'Fixed'],[.5,-.8,'Variable']],title='Resistor symbols')
add('5.3-01-M04','A lamp symbol is a circle containing a cross.','Its two leads connect into the circuit path.','Do not confuse the cross with an unconnected crossing of wires: it is inside the lamp’s circle and identifies the component. A lamp has a current-voltage behaviour that can differ from a fixed-temperature resistor because its filament heats.','Draw a lamp symbol with two connecting leads.','Draw a circle containing a cross.~Add a lead from each side into the circuit path.')
g('5.3-01-M04',[[[-1.5,0],[-.5,0]],[[.5,0],[1.5,0]],[[-.35,-.35],[.35,.35]],[[-.35,.35],[.35,-.35]]],circles=[dict(centre=[0,0],radius=.5)],title='Lamp')
add('5.3-01-M05','An ammeter symbol is a circle containing A; a voltmeter is a circle containing V.','Their connection positions differ even though their circle forms look similar.','Ammeter in series measures the branch current. Voltmeter in parallel measures a difference between two nodes. Label the letters clearly and connect both leads. The symbol alone does not show that the ammeter has low resistance or the voltmeter high resistance, but the circuit interpretation uses those ideal properties.','Draw ammeter and voltmeter symbols with their identifying letters.','Draw a circle marked A with two leads.~Draw a second circle marked V with two leads.')
g('5.3-01-M05',[[[-2,0],[-1.5,0]],[[-.5,0],[0,0]],[[1,0],[1.5,0]],[[2.5,0],[3,0]]],circles=[dict(centre=[-1,0],radius=.5),dict(centre=[2,0],radius=.5)],labels=[[-1.15,-.1,'A'],[1.85,-.1,'V']],title='Measuring instruments')
add('5.3-01-M06','A fuse symbol identifies a sacrificial overcurrent link in its circuit path.','A relay symbol shows a coil controlling a mechanically linked switch in a separate path.','Draw the fuse as a small rectangle with its link line through it in the chosen school convention. For a relay, keep the coil circuit electrically distinct from the contacts and use a dashed mechanical link. Energising the coil changes the switch position; it does not imply a direct electrical junction between the two circuits.','Draw a fuse symbol and a relay showing its coil and separate switch contacts.','Draw a small rectangular fuse with a through-line.~Show a relay coil and separate switch linked mechanically by a dashed line.')
