add('5.3-02','A steady series circuit has the same current through each component.','Charge is conserved rather than used up in successive components.','If two lamps in one unbranched loop carry 0.30 A, both have that current, although their energy transfers may differ. A resistor can reduce the whole circuit current, not remove some current permanently between its two ends. This statement assumes the steady circuit rather than a transient charge build-up.','Calculate current through the second lamp in a steady unbranched series loop when the first carries 0.30 A.','Charge conservation gives I2 = I1.~The second lamp carries 0.30 A.')
add('5.3-03','At a steady junction, total entering current equals total leaving current.','For two parallel load branches, source current is I1 + I2.','Draw arrows before writing the sum. If 0.20 A and 0.35 A flow outward through two branches, the incoming source supplies 0.55 A. Branch current is not necessarily equal unless branch properties make it so. Recombining the branches restores the same total return current in the simple loop.','Calculate source current for parallel branches carrying 0.25 A and 0.40 A.','I = I1 + I2 = 0.25 + 0.40.~The source current is 0.65 A.')
add('5.3-04','Series potential differences add to the supply potential difference around the simple load loop.','This expresses energy-per-charge accounting.','With a 9 V ideal supply and two series loads having drops of 4 V and 5 V, the total transfer per coulomb equals the source’s 9 J/C contribution. Equal current does not require equal voltage drops. Ideal connecting wires have negligible drops in this model.','Calculate the second series voltage drop when a 12 V supply gives 5 V across the first of two components.','V2 = 12 − 5.~The second drop is 7 V.')
add('5.3-05','Parallel branches connected to the same two nodes share the same potential difference.','Their currents can differ because their component properties differ.','A 6 V ideal supply directly across two resistor branches provides 6 V to each. It is not divided into 3 V per branch. Trace both endpoints: branches share voltage when they genuinely span the same two nodes, not merely because they look side by side on paper.','State the potential difference across a branch directly parallel to a 6 V ideal supply.','6 V.')
add('5.3-06','For positive resistances in parallel, total resistance is smaller than each individual branch resistance.','Additional paths increase total current at the same applied voltage.','Two 8 Ω resistors in parallel give 4 Ω. The supply drives twice the current compared with one alone. This is a different rule from series addition; the topology determines which calculation is valid. A short-circuit idealisation is a special limiting case, not an ordinary positive-resistance branch.','State how the resistance of two positive resistors in parallel compares with either one alone.','It is smaller than either branch resistance.')
add('5.3-07','Series resistances add directly, while parallel conductances add.','The parallel rule is 1/RT = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + … for positive-resistance branches.','Identify whether components share the same current path or the same two endpoints. In mixed circuits, simplify a genuine series or parallel subnetwork before combining the next stage. Do not apply a rule based solely on how close two symbols appear on a diagram.','Explain how circuit connections determine the resistance-combination rule.','Link one series path to RT = R1 + R2 + ….~For parallel paths at the same voltage, add reciprocal resistances and then invert the sum.')
add('5.3-07-M01','Resistances in one series path add.','RT = R1 + R2 for two ideal resistors.','The same current passes through both and their voltage drops add: V = IR1 + IR2 = I(R1 + R2). This explains the rule rather than treating it as a disconnected memory fact. Adding another positive series resistance reduces current from an unchanged ideal voltage source.','Calculate total resistance for 4 Ω and 7 Ω in series.','RT = 4 + 7.~The total is 11 Ω.')
add('5.3-07-M02','Parallel resistances combine through the reciprocal rule.','For two resistors, RT = R1R2/(R1 + R2).','For 6 Ω and 3 Ω, 1/RT = 1/6 + 1/3 = 0.5, so RT = 2 Ω. Invert the final reciprocal sum; 0.5 here is conductance in reciprocal ohms, not resistance in ohms. The total smaller than the smallest branch is a useful magnitude check.','Calculate the resistance of 6 Ω and 3 Ω in parallel.','1/RT = 1/6 + 1/3 = 0.5.~The total resistance is 2 Ω.')
add('5.3-08','Matched ideal cells connected parallel-aiding have the same voltage as one cell.','They can share the load current and provide greater charge capacity in the simple model.','This differs from series-aiding connection, which adds source voltages. Real cells must be appropriately matched and designed for parallel use; mismatched voltages can drive unwanted currents between them. The lesson uses ideal identical sources rather than instructing learners to combine arbitrary batteries.','Explain the advantage of suitable identical sources in parallel for a fixed-voltage load.','Their common voltage remains that of one source.~They can share current demand and provide a longer operating time in the matched ideal model.')
add('5.3-09','Adding series-aiding identical cells increases ideal source voltage.','For a fixed ohmic external resistance and negligible internal effects, greater voltage gives greater current.','Two ideal 1.5 V cells provide 3 V, compared with 1.5 V for one. At fixed R, I = V/R doubles. A lamp may heat and change resistance, so its actual current need not double exactly. Extra cells also must not exceed component ratings.','Explain why adding a series-aiding cell can increase current through a fixed resistor.','It adds source energy per charge and increases the applied voltage.~At fixed resistance, I = V/R therefore increases.')
add('5.3-10','A variable resistor controls current, a fuse interrupts excessive current and a relay provides electrically operated switching.','Their circuit functions differ even when each appears as a two-terminal part in a simplified path.','Use a rheostat to vary resistance, a correctly rated fuse for the intended overcurrent protection and a relay when one circuit needs to operate a separate contact path. A fuse is not a reusable variable resistor. A relay’s coil and contacts perform different roles and may have different ratings.','Explain the different functions of a variable resistor and relay in a low-voltage control circuit.','Link the variable resistor to changing current by changing resistance.~The relay uses coil energisation to operate a separate switch.')
for key,core,detail,teach,prompt,answer in [
('5.3-10-M01','A rheostat varies the resistance included in a circuit.','With other conditions fixed, a greater series resistance reduces current.','Choose a suitable current rating and start with a limiting setting. In a classroom circuit it allows several V-I readings without replacing the test conductor.','State one use of a variable resistor.','To adjust circuit current by changing resistance.'),
('5.3-10-M02','A fuse is intended to melt and break its path if excessive current persists.','Its rating must match the protected circuit and expected normal operation.','The protection depends on heating of the link and its time-current response. Replacing it with a wire defeats the designed protection.','State the purpose of a fuse.','To interrupt excessive current by melting its link.'),
('5.3-10-M03','A relay uses an electromagnet to operate switch contacts.','A control circuit can operate a separate load circuit.','Coil current produces magnetic attraction that moves an armature and changes the contact state. Removing current lets a spring restore the normal state in a basic relay.','State one use of a relay.','To let a control circuit operate a separate switched circuit.'),
('ANNEX.D-01','A diode symbol has a directed triangular element facing a bar representing the cathode side in the chosen school convention.','In its observed rectification role it conducts much more readily forward than reverse in the intended range.','Conventional forward current goes from the anode side toward the cathode bar. Recognise the symbol and one-direction use; no semiconductor-junction theory is required. This limited annex supports the observed characteristic taught above.','State the function of a diode in simple rectification.','It allows appreciable current mainly in one direction.')]:add(key,core,detail,teach,prompt,answer)
